  "content": "<p><strong>Unit 18 - Angles — Title &amp; Goal</strong><br />Comprehensive lesson covering Unit 18 - Angles for SAT Math preparation.</p>\n\n<p>In a triangle, the <strong>exterior angle</strong> is equal to the sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles.</p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mtext>Exterior Angle</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>Remote Interior Angle 1</mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext>Remote Interior Angle 2</mtext></mrow></math>\n<p><strong>Example 1</strong><br/>\nIn a triangle, one exterior angle is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>. If one remote interior angle is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>, the other is:  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>°</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>°</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>80</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>\n<p>Try these on your own. Check your steps, not just the final answer.</p>\n<p>1) In a triangle, one angle is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>50</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math> and another is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Find the exterior angle adjacent to the third angle.\n2) Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. If one angle is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>, find its alternate interior angle.\n3) Find the sum of interior angles of a decagon.\n<p>When a transversal cuts two parallel lines:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corresponding angles</strong> are equal.  </li>\n<li><strong>Alternate interior angles</strong> are equal.  </li>\n<li><strong>Same-side interior angles</strong> are supplementary.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 2</strong><br/>\nIf one angle is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>, then the corresponding angle is also <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Interior Angle Sum</strong></p>\n<p>For an <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-sided polygon:  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mtext>Sum of interior angles</mtext><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>×</mi><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>\n<p><strong>Each Interior Angle (regular polygon)</strong></p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>×</mi><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>\n<p><strong>Each Exterior Angle (regular polygon)</strong></p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>\n<p><strong>Example 3</strong><br />A hexagon (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>):</p><ul><li>Interior angle sum <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>×</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>720</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math></li><li>Each interior angle <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>720</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math></li></ul>\n<hr />\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complementary angles</strong>: sum <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math> </li>\n<li><strong>Supplementary angles</strong>: sum <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math> </li>\n<li><strong>Vertical angles</strong>: equal  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 4</strong><br/>\nIf two supplementary angles are in ratio <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>:</mi><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, find them.  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mn>9</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>\n<p>Angles = <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>°</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>140</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math> </p>\n<hr />\n<ul>\n<li>Always check for parallel lines → look for equal angles.  </li>\n<li>For polygons, memorize <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>×</mi><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </li>\n<li>Exterior angle theorem is very common.  </li>\n<li>Watch out for “trick” wording with supplements/complements.  </li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p>1) In a triangle, one angle is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>50</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math> and another is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Find the exterior angle adjacent to the third angle<br/>\n2) Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. If one angle is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>, find its alternate interior angle<br/>\n3) Find the sum of interior angles of a decagon<br/>\n4) Find each interior angle of a regular octagon<br/>\n5) Two angles are complementary. One is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, the other is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Solve for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<hr />\n<p>1) Third angle <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Exterior angle = <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>70</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>110</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>2) Alternate interior angle = <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>3) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>×</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>×</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>1440</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>4) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>×</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>×</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>108</mn><mo>°</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>5) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p><strong>More practice</strong></p>\n<p>Keep going—aim for accuracy first, then speed.</p>\n<p><strong>Mixed practice set</strong></p>\n<p>These mix everything above. Time yourself and reflect on any mistakes.</p>\n4) Find each interior angle of a regular octagon.\n5) Two angles are complementary. One is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, the other is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Solve for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.",
